13:["$","aside",null,{"className":"docs_toc__B_lbZ","aria-label":"On this page","children":[["$","strong",null,{"children":"ON THIS PAGE"}],[["$","a","start-here-what-is-a-queue",{"href":"#start-here-what-is-a-queue","children":"Start here: what is a queue?"}],["$","a","who-this-is-for",{"href":"#who-this-is-for","children":"Who this is for"}],["$","a","what-you-will-be-able-to-do",{"href":"#what-you-will-be-able-to-do","children":"What you will be able to do"}],["$","a","learning-approach",{"href":"#learning-approach","children":"Learning approach"}],["$","a","diagram-the-delivery-and-recovery-path",{"href":"#diagram-the-delivery-and-recovery-path","children":"Diagram: the delivery and recovery path"}],["$","a","lesson-index",{"href":"#lesson-index","children":"Lesson index"}],["$","a","completion-check",{"href":"#completion-check","children":"Completion check"}],["$","a","primary-references",{"href":"#primary-references","children":"Primary references"}]]]}]
14:["$","main",null,{"className":"docs_main__7o2m6","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"docs_articleMeta__8zYHO","children":[["$","span",null,{"children":"MODULE OVERVIEW"}],["$","span",null,{"children":"Systems"}]]}],["$","article",null,{"className":"docs_article__Ja_Vh","children":[["$","$1","0",{"children":[[["$","h1","h1-0",{"children":"Queues and reliable delivery"}],"\n",["$","p","p-0",{"children":"A queue is a place to hold work until a worker is ready to process it. For example, a website can accept a document export request, put an export job in a queue, and let a background worker create the file while the user continues using the site. This module begins with that basic flow, then explains the failure and scaling decisions needed to run it reliably."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-0",{"id":"start-here-what-is-a-queue","children":"Start here: what is a queue?"}],"\n",["$","p","p-1",{"children":["Think of a queue as a waiting line for tasks. A ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"producer"}]," adds a message describing a task; a ",["$","strong","strong-1",{"children":"worker"}]," (also called a consumer) takes a message and performs it. The queue is usually managed by a ",["$","strong","strong-2",{"children":"broker"}]," or queue service. Unlike a person waiting in line, the website can respond before the task is finished. That is ",["$","strong","strong-3",{"children":"asynchronous processing"}],"."]}],"\n",["$","figure","p-2",{"className":"docs_studyFigure__4Js_y","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"docs_studyFigureViewport__ZYDNN","children":["$","img","img-0",{"className":"docs_studyFigureImage__Gb2YJ","src":"/learning/async-systems/queue-basics.svg","alt":"A user asks for an export; the API produces a job, the queue holds it, and a worker creates the file.","width":"920","height":"402","loading":"lazy"}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":"A user asks for an export; the API produces a job, the queue holds it, and a worker creates the file."}]]}],"\n",["$","details","pre-0",{"className":"docs_textDiagram__KfHuf","children":[["$","summary",null,{"children":"Read the text version of this flow"}],["$","pre",null,{"children":["$","code","code-0",{"className":"language-aosdf-diagram","children":"User request ──▶ API (producer) ──▶ Queue ──▶ Worker (consumer) ──▶ Finished export\n                    │\n                    └──▶ returns an operation ID so the user can check progress\n"}]}]]}],"\n",["$","p","p-3",{"children":"Queues are useful when work is slow, can happen later, arrives in bursts, or needs independent retries. Common examples include document exports, image processing, email delivery, and importing files. A queue adds moving parts, so a short, fast operation that must finish before a response may be simpler as a direct call. A queue alone does not guarantee a task runs once, finishes in order, or succeeds; the later lessons show how to define those promises."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-1",{"id":"who-this-is-for","children":"Who this is for"}],"\n",["$","p","p-4",{"children":"People new to background jobs and engineers building or reviewing event consumers, scheduled tasks, webhooks, or live progress updates. The first lesson assumes only basic familiarity with a website request. Later lessons use HTTP and database transactions, defining the queue terms as they appear. No specific broker is required; the examples use an at-least-once queue such as an Amazon SQS standard queue, and provider-specific behavior is called out where relevant."}],"\n",["$","h2","h2-2",{"id":"what-you-will-be-able-to-do","children":"What you will be able to do"}],"\n",["$","ul","ul-0",{"children":["\n",["$","li","li-0",{"children":"Explain what a queue, producer, message, and worker do, and when a queue is useful."}],"\n","$L20","\n","$L21","\n","$L22","\n","$L23","\n","$L24","\n","$L25","\n","$L26","\n"]}],"\n","$L27","\n","$L28","\n","$L29","\n","$L2a","\n","$L2b","\n","$L2c"],"$L2d"]}],"$L2e"]}],"$L2f"]}]
