20:["$","li","li-1",{"children":"Define what an API promises when it accepts asynchronous work and how a client checks completion."}]
21:["$","li","li-2",{"children":"Write a job contract with an operation ID, version, owner, attempt policy, and safe acknowledgement point."}]
22:["$","li","li-3",{"children":"Estimate queue growth and choose backpressure before overload damages downstream services."}]
23:["$","li","li-4",{"children":"Classify failures, set timeouts and retry budgets, quarantine poison messages, and plan replay."}]
24:["$","li","li-5",{"children":"Prevent a duplicate delivery from repeating an external effect."}]
25:["$","li","li-6",{"children":"Choose between a work queue, pub/sub event, outbox, saga, webhook, or browser update based on the communication contract."}]
26:["$","li","li-7",{"children":"Operate the system with queue age, result state, tracing, alerts, and a recovery runbook."}]
27:["$","h2","h2-3",{"id":"learning-approach","children":"Learning approach"}]
28:["$","p","p-5",{"children":["Follow one general example: a user requests a ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"document export"}],". The API records an export operation and returns ",["$","code","code-0",{"children":"202 Accepted"}]," with its ID. A worker creates a file and marks the operation complete. The UI polls or receives a progress update. If generation fails, the user should see an honest status and an operator should be able to diagnose or replay the job safely."]}]
29:["$","p","p-6",{"children":["If queues are new to you, read ",["$","$L11","a-0",{"href":"/learn/ai-engineer-fde/async-systems/what-is-a-queue","children":"What a queue is and when to use one"}]," before the engineering contracts. If you already operate workers, use that lesson to check your vocabulary and move into the failure cases. After each ",["$","strong","strong-0",{"children":"Try it"}],", add one section to a short design note: queue choice, request contract, job schema, capacity estimate, retry table, idempotency rule, event boundary, and runbook. The final lesson checks that these sections agree."]}]
2a:["$","h2","h2-4",{"id":"diagram-the-delivery-and-recovery-path","children":"Diagram: the delivery and recovery path"}]
2b:["$","p","p-7",{"children":"The main path records intent, publishes work, claims a job, applies an effect, and acknowledges completion. A transient failure follows a bounded retry path; repeated failures move to a dead-letter queue for inspection. The diagram is a teaching model: a real system must also handle a worker crash between the effect and acknowledgement."}]
2c:["$","figure","p-8",{"className":"docs_studyFigure__4Js_y","children":[["$","div",null,{"className":"docs_studyFigureViewport__ZYDNN","children":["$","img","img-0",{"className":"docs_studyFigureImage__Gb2YJ","src":"/learning/async-systems/acceptance.svg","alt":"The API records an operation, sends it to a queue, and a worker creates a result after returning a status ID to the user.","width":"920","height":"402","loading":"lazy"}]}],["$","figcaption",null,{"children":"The API records an operation, sends it to a queue, and a worker creates a result after returning a status ID to the user."}]]}]
